Pericarditis
Cardiovascular
Pleuritic chest pain relieved by sitting forward, Pericardial friction rub, Widespread ST elevation/PR depression

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CDV-032
Reason for Visit: Sharp, severe chest pain that worsens with breathing and lying flat.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a patient with sharp, positional chest pain. This presentation requires immediate clinical evaluation to distinguish between acute pericarditis, a pulmonary embolism, or an atypical presentation of acute coronary syndrome. Assessment could not safely wait until the next available in-hours appointment due to the severe nature of the pain and the critical need to rule out life-threatening cardiopulmonary emergencies.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "There is a sharp, stabbing pain in the center of my chest that gets agonizing if I take a deep breath or try to lie down."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a 24-hour history of sharp, pleuritic, retrosternal chest pain. The pain is severely exacerbated by lying flat in bed and taking a deep inspiration. Patient notes the pain is significantly relieved by sitting up and leaning forward. The pain occasionally radiates to the trapezius ridges. Patient reports a recent history of a viral prodrome (sore throat and mild myalgias) approximately one week ago. KEY SYMPTOMS: Sharp, pleuritic, retrosternal chest pain. Pain exacerbated by lying flat and deep inspiration. Pain significantly relieved by sitting up and leaning forward. Radiation of pain to the trapezius ridges. Recent history of a viral prodrome.
Functional Status: Independent, currently sitting rigidly upright on the edge of the sofa.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; unable to sleep due to pain when supine.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 120/75 | HR: 105 | Temp: 37.8 C | RR: 20 | SpO2: 98 percent | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Uncomfortable, leaning forward, taking shallow breaths to avoid splinting pain.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Tachycardia out of proportion to the low-grade fever. A high-pitched, scratching, triphasic pericardial friction rub is distinctly audible at the left lower sternal border, loudest when the patient leans forward in expiration. No S3/S4 gallops. No elevated jugular venous pressure. Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. No calf tenderness or swelling. DIAGNOSTIC TETRAD: Pleuritic chest pain relieved by sitting forward, Pericardial friction rub, Widespread ST elevation/PR depression (anticipated on formal 12-lead ECG).
Environmental Observations: Patient sleeping in a recliner chair due to inability to lie flat.
Medication Reconciliation: Taken paracetamol with minimal relief of chest pain.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Viral Pericarditis - Status: Exacerbated (Acute Phase)
Differentials:

Pulmonary Embolism - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of a classic pericardial friction rub, relief of pain by sitting forward, and radiation to the trapezius ridge, which are highly specific to pericardial inflammation rather than a vascular clot.

Acute Myocardial Infarction - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The pain is sharp and pleuritic rather than crushing, highly positional (relieved by sitting forward), and preceded by a viral illness.

Costochondritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of a friction rub, fever, and the profound positional nature of the pain (supine vs. sitting), rather than pain simply reproducible by chest wall palpation.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the pathognomonic features of acute pericarditis: sharp, pleuritic chest pain radiating to the trapezius, highly responsive to position (worse supine, better leaning forward), and a distinct pericardial friction rub following a viral illness. Urgent after-hours assessment was mandatory to identify the friction rub, clinically rule out an acute coronary syndrome or massive pulmonary embolism, and initiate high-dose anti-inflammatory therapy to manage the severe, disabling pain, avoiding an unnecessary emergency department presentation while ensuring patient safety.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed high-dose non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (e.g., Ibuprofen 600mg TDS with food) and a proton pump inhibitor for gastric protection. Arranged a non-urgent 12-lead ECG and basic blood work (CRP, troponin) via regular GP to formally confirm widespread ST elevation and rule out concurrent myocarditis.
Positive Behavioral Change: Instructed the patient to take their anti-inflammatory medication strictly with a full meal and a glass of milk. By coupling the potent medication with significant food intake, the patient actively protects their gastric lining from ulceration. The reward is rapid, sustained relief of the severe chest pain without developing severe stomach cramps.
Home-Based Services: None.
DME/Safety: None.
Follow-up: Urgent General Practice review in 24-48 hours to review ECG, blood markers, and consider the addition of Colchicine.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the pain changes character to a heavy, crushing sensation, if the patient develops severe shortness of breath at rest, or experiences fainting or swelling in the legs, they must call an ambulance immediately.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis relies on classic clinical signs; formal ECG and blood tests are required to confirm severity and rule out muscle involvement (myocarditis); conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Viral inflammation of the heart lining is intensely painful but generally benign and self-limiting with appropriate, scheduled anti-inflammatory therapy.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Sleep propped up. Take medications strictly with food. Seek emergency care immediately if you develop crushing chest pain or severe breathlessness.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The recent virus has caused inflammation in the sac surrounding your heart. When you lie flat or breathe deeply, the inflamed layers rub together, causing the sharp pain and the scratching sound I heard. High-dose anti-inflammatories will quickly settle this inflammation.
Actionable Steps: Start the Ibuprofen immediately with a meal. Continue to sleep in the recliner chair tonight. See your GP tomorrow for an ECG.

